# Feature branches

When starting work that changes or improves the app, create a **new branch from `main`** named for that feature — not a dump of mixed work.

## Naming

Use `feature/<area>-<short-goal>` (kebab-case):

| Area | Examples |
|------|----------|
| Scale studio | `feature/scale-detection`, `feature/scale-session-history`, `feature/scale-coaching` |
| Violin tuner | `feature/tuner-meter`, `feature/tuner-mic` |
| Tuning trainer | `feature/tuning-trainer-results`, `feature/tuning-trainer-record` |
| Cross-cutting UI | `feature/ui-<what>` or `Mus-UI-alterations` for a named UI pass |
| Piece practice (Phase 2+) | `feature/piece-<name>` |

Do **not** pile unrelated fixes onto an old feature branch. Finish or abandon that PR first, then branch again from updated `main`.

## Workflow

1. `git checkout main && git pull`
2. `git checkout -b feature/<area>-<short-goal>`
3. Commit only that feature’s changes
4. Push and open a **pull request** into `main` (GitHub’s name for a merge request)
5. After merge, delete the feature branch locally and on the remote

When a major task is finished or the user moves on, follow **ship-feature-to-main** so the PR is opened promptly and merged into `main` when there are no conflicts.

## This repo’s current UI pass

`Mus-UI-alterations` is the branch for the warm redesign / navigation polish across pages. Do not rename it to a single product area (scale/tuner) — that work is intentionally cross-cutting.
